1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186287909 "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot {r}^{2}+(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}-{r}^{2}-12 \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =6 \\\\ r & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\sqrt{6}.$"] [17592186287928 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{26}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287929 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{\\sqrt{22}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287930 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}-4\\sqrt{21}}{\\sqrt{126}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287931 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c-{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{11}{3}-\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =11(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =11(\\frac{5}{15}-\\frac{3}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287932 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3+\\sqrt{11}\\cdot \\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{13}{6}-\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =13(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =13(\\frac{2}{12}-\\frac{3}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287933 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p+{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =9(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{2}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287968 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))i-(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,u\\times v\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287969 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\times g & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-2))j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =5i-7j-k \\\\  & =⟨5,-7,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,f\\times g\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287970 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h\\times l & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-3i+6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,6,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,h\\times l\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287971 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(2i-2j+2k)-(3i-2j+k) \\\\  & =-i+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(i-3j+3k)-(3i-2j+k) \\\\  & =-2i-j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)i-((-1)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1)j+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =i+k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186290429 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b753318ebad9270c3fc8788d0e3949ffb29a800eb3f8dd6fb67cb271be68033.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 64=256.\n$$"]}
